// tests/kernel/driver/death_witness.pdx — R31.M0-1583 (#1583)
//
// Boot witness: process death is wired to the R29 cascade.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this fixture is for
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// R29 (#1044/#1047/#1048) built a complete cascade-restart and capability-
// revocation mechanism. Its entry point, driver_restart_node, had five call
// sites and every one of them was synthetic — the chaos harness and the boot
// witness. No fault handler and no supervisor reached it. A driver process
// could die holding every capability it was ever granted and nothing would
// take them back.
//
// That is leaked AUTHORITY, not leaked memory. A capability slot outliving
// its owner is inherited by whatever process next occupies it. Leaked memory
// is recovered by reboot; leaked authority is exercised first.
//
// So the property under test is not "the mechanism works" — R29's own
// witness already shows that, and re-showing it would be restatement. It is
// that DEATH REACHES THE MECHANISM, by both routes, and that reaching it is
// safe to do twice and refuses to do it to the wrong process.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The three ways this could be got wrong, and where each is asserted
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   * THE FAULT PATH DOES NOT FIRE. An orderly exit is the case LEAST
//     likely to be holding anything dangerous; a process that calls exit has
//     usually finished its work. The process that faults mid-transaction is
//     the one holding an OpRegion window or an I2C claim. A witness that
//     covered only sys_exit would be green while the real hazard stayed
//     untouched. Sub-tests E–J drive fault_ring3_death_check itself, through
//     a synthetic ring-3 trap frame, and assert the full revocation — and
//     sub-tests C/D first assert that its four refusals still refuse, so
//     "it fired" is distinguishable from "it fires at everything".
//
//   * A RECYCLED PID REVOKES A LIVE PROCESS. pid_alloc is a dense-low-first
//     scan and pid_free zeroes the slot, so a reaped pid goes to the very
//     next task_new — immediately, with no aging. Correlating on the number
//     alone would eventually tear down a LIVE process's authority, which is
//     strictly worse than the leak being fixed. Sub-test M manufactures a
//     STALE BINDING — a row naming a real, currently-live pid at a
//     generation that no longer exists, which no audited path can produce —
//     and asserts the death of that pid does not touch it.
//
//   * A SECOND REVOCATION CORRUPTS THE FIRST. Death, an explicit teardown
//     and a supervisor sweep can all reach one row. Sub-tests K and L call
//     the notifier again on a row it has already consumed and assert it
//     resolves nothing AND that the budget was not charged a second time —
//     because five spurious charges latch perm_failed on a node whose only
//     fault was dying once.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Sub-tests
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   0  Idempotent pre-clean of the slots and endpoints this fixture uses.
//   1  Register slot 20, drive it Init -> Running, bind a client endpoint.
//   2  driver_death_bind(20, pid) installs the key; both halves read back.
//   3  driver_death_bind refuses a pid that hosts no task (BIND_NO_TASK).
//   4  driver_death_find_slot answers on the exact key, and ONLY on it:
//      wrong pid, wrong generation, zero pid and zero generation all miss.
//   C  fault_ring3_death_check DECLINES a ring-0 frame — a kernel fault
//      must still panic.
//   D  ...and declines with no current task, with pid 1 (init), and with a
//      task already ZOMBIE.
//   E  The ring-3 frame is accepted: TERMINATED.
//   F  The task is ZOMBIE with a fault-shaped exit status (0x100 | vector),
//      distinguishable from any status sys_exit can carry.
//   G  The row's binding was consumed — pid and generation both read back 0.
//   H  ChannelDead: the endpoint bound to slot 20 carries the dead latch,
//      and the client parked on it was woken to RUNNABLE.
//   I  Auto-restart: slot 20 is back at Init on a FRESH incarnation.
//   J  The budget was charged exactly once.
//   K  A second notify for the same pid resolves nothing (NO_SLOT).
//   L  ...and did not charge the budget again, and did not bump the
//      incarnation again.
//   M  THE RECYCLED-PID CASE. A stale binding — live pid, dead generation —
//      is not matched, and the row it names is untouched by that pid's
//      death.
//   N  The sys_exit route: a second slot bound to a second task is torn
//      down by sys_exit_body itself, with no fault involved.
//   P  THE UNBIND IS LOAD-BEARING ON ITS OWN. Six deaths exhaust the
//      restart budget; on the escalating one no re-arm runs, so the
//      binding being gone afterwards can only be the notifier's own
//      unbind. Without this, G/K/L are satisfied by EITHER of the two
//      mechanisms that drop a binding and cannot tell you one was lost.
//   Q  ...and a second arrival at the escalated node resolves NOTHING,
//      rather than resolving the row and being turned away by the
//      perm_failed latch.
//   O  Leave the driver table, the endpoint table and _current_tcb as we
//      found them.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why a synthetic trap frame rather than a real fault
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// fault_ring3_death_check performs the revocation and RETURNS; the handler
// does the four-call scheduling tail. The witness drives the returning half
// with a frame it builds and a task it owns, which is what lets it assert on
// the interesting part — the revocation — rather than on the fact that a
// fault occurred.
//
// The tail it cannot drive (runq_dequeue, sched_pick_next_r15,
// sched_switch_r15) is byte-identical to dispatch_exit's, which every shell
// smoke mode runs on every `exit`. What remains unwitnessed is a genuine
// CPU-raised ring-3 fault arriving at the handler; that is recorded in
// design/drivers/process-death.md §7 rather than papered over.
//
// This follows the ghost-row precedent set by tests/kernel/cap/
// gpio_cap_synth.pdx sub-test L: manufacture a state no API can produce,
// then assert the machinery collects it. Sub-test M is that pattern applied
// to a stale pid binding.

module DeathWitness = structure {

  // 16 bytes, NUL-padded — driver_table_register copies exactly two u64s
  // from this address, so the buffer must be exactly 16 readable bytes.
  pub let pdw_name : [u8; 16] = "death-witness\0\0\0"

  pub let mut _pdw_stage    : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _pdw_saved_tcb: u64 = 0
  pub let mut _pdw_pid      : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _pdw_gen      : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _pdw_ep       : u64 = 0

  // A task_struct-shaped scratch region (2224 bytes = 278 u64s), the same
  // shape R29's witness uses. A real task_new would allocate an address
  // space and consume a pid slot for the rest of boot; the fields the death
  // path reads are pid @+0, parent_pid @+4, state @+8 and exit_status @+12,
  // and all four are written here explicitly.
  pub let mut _pdw_tcb      : [u64; 278] = uninit @align(8)

  // The client parked on the driver's endpoint. Sub-test H asserts the
  // cascade woke it, which is the client-visible half of ChannelDead.
  pub let mut _pdw_client   : [u64; 278] = uninit @align(8)

  // A synthetic trap frame. Only CS at +144 is read by the policy, but the
  // frame is declared at full width (22 qwords = 176 bytes) so the offset
  // arithmetic is the real arithmetic and not a shortened stand-in.
  pub let mut _pdw_frame    : [u64; 22]  = uninit @align(8)

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdw_frame_cs(cs) -> u64
  //
  // Build a trap frame carrying `cs`, and return its address. Everything
  // else in the frame is zero, which is legal: the policy reads CS and
  // nothing else, and leaving the rest zero makes it obvious in a failure
  // that no other field was consulted.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let pdw_frame_cs : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (cs: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): stamp a code-segment selector into the synthetic trap frame at FAULT_FRAME_OFF_CS (+144) and answer the frame address. The frame is zeroed first, in full, so a policy that started consulting a second field would be reading a zero it did not choose rather than a stale value from the previous sub-test — a decline caused by leftover state would otherwise look identical to a decline caused by the gate under test. Leaf, no nested calls; rax/rcx/r8 scratch only. Reserved labels: prefixed pdw_.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _pdw_frame];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      pdw_frame_zero:
        cmp rcx, 22;
        jae pdw_frame_stamp;
        mov r8, 0;
        mov [rax + rcx*8], r8;
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp pdw_frame_zero;
      pdw_frame_stamp:
        mov [rax + 144], rdi;              // FAULT_FRAME_OFF_CS
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdw_tcb_init(tcb, pid, state) -> ()
  //
  // Write the four task_struct fields the death path reads, and nothing
  // else. parent_pid is forced to 0 so sys_exit_body's wake path stops at
  // its step-3 gate rather than reaching into a real task's slab.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let pdw_tcb_init : (u64, u64, u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (tcb: u64) (pid: u64) (state: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): initialise a synthetic task_struct — pid (u32 @+0), parent_pid (u32 @+4, forced ZERO), state (u32 @+8), exit_status (u32 @+12, zeroed). parent_pid = 0 is load-bearing and not tidiness: sys_exit_body's step 3 returns immediately on a zero parent, so the wake path never dereferences _pid_table and this fixture cannot perturb a real task's state through the parent-wakeup arm. exit_status is cleared so sub-test F's assertion on the fault-shaped status is proving a write rather than reading a value that was already there. Leaf; rax scratch only. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov [rdi + 0],  esi;               // pid
        xor eax, eax;
        mov [rdi + 4],  eax;               // parent_pid = 0
        mov [rdi + 8],  edx;               // state
        xor eax, eax;
        mov [rdi + 12], eax;               // exit_status = 0
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let pdw_set_current : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (tcb: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): point _current_tcb at a task this fixture owns, and put it back afterwards. fault_ring3_death_check reads _current_tcb because that is how the real handlers identify the faulting task, and a witness that passed the TCB as an argument instead would be testing a different function from the one the IDT reaches. Sub-test O restores the saved value, so the substitution does not outlive the fixture. Leaf; no calls. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _current_tcb];
        mov [rax], rdi;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let pdw_get_current : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): read _current_tcb so sub-test 0 can save it and sub-test O can prove it was restored. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _current_tcb];
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let pdw_state : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (tcb: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): read a task_struct's state (u32 @+8). Every 'the task died' and 'the client woke' assertion goes through this one accessor so a state change asserted with a differently-shaped read cannot pass by accident. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov eax, [rdi + 8];
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let pdw_exit_status : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (tcb: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): read a task_struct's exit_status (u32 @+12). Sub-test F asserts the fault-shaped encoding FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE | vector, which is what distinguishes 'died on #GP' from 'exited 13' to a parent that reaps the corpse. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov eax, [rdi + 12];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // process_death_witness() -> u64
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let death_witness : () -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{boot, cap, sched} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {boot, cap, sched},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): boot witness that process death reaches the R29 cascade, by both routes, idempotently, and refuses to reach it for a recycled pid. Five pushes (rbx/r12/r13/r14/r15) keep rsp %16 == 0 across every nested call; rbx = the driver slot under test, r12 = the pid bound to it, r13 = its generation, r14 = the endpoint id, r15 = comparison scratch. Failure is reported by klog_s1_d1 with the stage breadcrumb from _pdw_stage and the fixture continues, matching every other witness in the tree — detection is the ABSENCE of the OK line from the golden-ordered log, so a fixture that halted on failure would hide every later assertion. The driver slot chosen is 20, outside the 8..11 R29's cascade witness uses and outside the 0..2 the lifecycle witnesses use, so the two cannot interfere whatever order they run in. The pid chosen is 40, above every pid the ~23 boot witnesses allocate and below MAX_PIDS, so pid_gen_bump(40) stamps a generation no real task competes for. Sub-test M manufactures a STALE BINDING by calling driver_table_bind_pid directly with a dead generation — the storage primitive, not driver_death_bind, precisely because the audited path refuses to produce this state; that is the ghost-row pattern from tests/kernel/cap/gpio_cap_synth.pdx sub-test L applied to a pid key. _current_tcb is saved in sub-test 0 and restored in sub-test O, on both the success and the failure exit, so a failed assertion cannot leave the scheduler pointed at fixture storage.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;                          // 5 pushes -> rsp %16 = 0

        mov rbx, 20;                       // the driver slot under test

        // ================================================================
        // 0. Idempotent pre-clean, and save the scheduler's current task.
        // ================================================================
        xor rax, rax;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        call pdw_get_current;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_saved_tcb];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_unregister;
        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_table_unregister;
        mov rdi, 22;
        call driver_table_unregister;

        // ================================================================
        // 1. Register slot 20, drive it Running, give it a client channel.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 1;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 40;                       // pid — see the justification
        lea rdx, [rip + pdw_name];
        xor rcx, rcx;
        call driver_table_register;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 1;                        // DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
        call driver_lifecycle_transition;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        call endpoint_alloc;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;       // ENDPOINT_ALLOC_NONE
        cmp rax, r15;
        je  pdw_fail;
        mov r14, rax;                      // endpoint id
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_ep];
        mov [rcx], r14;

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, rbx;
        xor rdx, rdx;                      // incarnation 0
        call endpoint_bind_driver;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Park a client on it, pre-flipped to WAITING so sched_wake's #663
        // early-exit guard does not swallow the wake.
        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_client];
        mov rsi, 41;
        mov rdx, 2;                        // STATE_WAITING
        call pdw_tcb_init;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call endpoint_lookup;              // rax = row address
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  pdw_fail;
        lea r10, [rip + _pdw_client];
        mov [rax + 32], r10;               // ENDPOINT_OFF_WAITER_TCB

        // ================================================================
        // 2. Bind the row to a task incarnation.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 2;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, 40;
        call pid_gen_bump;                 // stamp a live incarnation
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  pdw_fail;
        mov r13, rax;                      // generation
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_gen];
        mov [rcx], r13;

        mov r12, 40;                       // pid
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_pid];
        mov [rcx], r12;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call driver_death_bind;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid;
        cmp rax, r12;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // 3. A pid that hosts no task cannot be bound. Binding one would
        //    install a key the NEXT occupant of that pid slot would match
        //    — the wrong-process revocation, manufactured at bind time.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 3;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, 21;
        mov rsi, 63;                       // never allocated: generation 0
        lea rdx, [rip + pdw_name];
        xor rcx, rcx;
        call driver_table_register;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 21;
        mov rsi, 63;
        call driver_death_bind;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFC3;               // DRV_DEATH_BIND_NO_TASK
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // refused, and nothing written
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // 4. The scan answers on the exact key and on nothing else.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 4;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        cmp rax, rbx;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Wrong generation, same pid — this is the recycled-pid refusal in
        // its simplest form.
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        add rsi, 1;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;       // DRV_DEATH_SLOT_NONE
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Wrong pid, same generation. Generations are PER-PID counters, so
        // a generation alone names nothing.
        mov rdi, r12;
        add rdi, 1;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Zero pid and zero generation are what an UNBOUND row reads as.
        // Without the up-front gates they would match the first free row.
        xor rdi, rdi;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, r12;
        xor rsi, rsi;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // C. A RING-0 FAULT MUST STILL PANIC.
        //
        //    This is the assertion that keeps the change from being a
        //    regression rather than a fix. A kernel-integrity failure that
        //    got converted into a task death would keep running on an
        //    invariant already observed broken — and would do it silently.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 5;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 40;
        mov rdx, 1;                        // STATE_RUNNABLE
        call pdw_tcb_init;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_set_current;

        mov rdi, 8;                        // ring-0 selector
        call pdw_frame_cs;
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, 13;                       // EXC_GP
        call fault_ring3_death_check;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // FAULT_KILL_DECLINE
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Declining must mean declining: the row is still bound.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // D. The other three refusals: no current task, init, and a task
        //    that is already dead.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 6;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        xor rdi, rdi;
        call pdw_set_current;              // no current task

        mov rdi, 27;                       // ring-3 selector (0x1B)
        call pdw_frame_cs;
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, 13;
        call fault_ring3_death_check;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 1;                        // init
        mov rdx, 1;
        call pdw_tcb_init;
        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_set_current;

        mov rdi, 27;
        call pdw_frame_cs;
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, 13;
        call fault_ring3_death_check;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 40;
        mov rdx, 3;                        // STATE_ZOMBIE — already dead
        call pdw_tcb_init;
        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_set_current;

        mov rdi, 27;
        call pdw_frame_cs;
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, 13;
        call fault_ring3_death_check;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Four refusals later, nothing has been revoked.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
        cmp rax, 1;                        // still DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // E. THE FAULT PATH FIRES.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 7;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 40;
        mov rdx, 1;                        // STATE_RUNNABLE — alive again
        call pdw_tcb_init;
        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_set_current;

        mov rdi, 27;                       // ring-3 selector
        call pdw_frame_cs;
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, 13;                       // EXC_GP
        call fault_ring3_death_check;
        cmp rax, 1;                        // FAULT_KILL_TERMINATED
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // F. The task is dead, and the corpse says how it died.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 8;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_state;
        cmp rax, 3;                        // STATE_ZOMBIE
        jne pdw_fail;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_exit_status;
        mov r15, 0x10D;                    // FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE | 13
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // G. The binding was CONSUMED. This is what makes a second
        //    revocation a no-op instead of a second charge.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 9;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // DRIVER_ROW_PIDGEN_UNBOUND
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // H. ChannelDead — the client-visible half. The endpoint carries
        //    the dead latch, and the client parked on it was woken. A
        //    cascade that killed the channel without waking the client
        //    would leave it blocked with no wake source remaining.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 10;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call endpoint_is_dead;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_client];
        call pdw_state;
        cmp rax, 1;                        // STATE_RUNNABLE — woken
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // I. Auto-restart: a NEW life, not a resurrected one.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 11;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // DRIVER_STATE_INIT
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_incarnation;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // J. The budget was charged exactly once.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 12;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_restart_count;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // K. IDEMPOTENCE. A fault handler and a supervisor sweep can both
        //    reach a dying process. The second arrival must resolve
        //    nothing.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 13;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call driver_death_notify;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFC1;               // DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // L. ...and charged nothing. Five spurious charges would latch
        //    perm_failed on a node whose only fault was dying once, so
        //    "returns cleanly" is not enough on its own.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 14;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_restart_count;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_incarnation;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // M. THE RECYCLED-PID CASE — the ghost-row pattern applied to a
        //    pid key.
        //
        //    Slot 21 is bound BY HAND to (pid 40, generation 1) — a pid
        //    that is live, at a generation that is not. driver_death_bind
        //    refuses to produce this state, which is exactly why the
        //    storage primitive is called directly: the guard has to be
        //    tested against a state the audited path cannot reach, or it
        //    is only being tested against itself.
        //
        //    Then pid 40 is advanced to a NEW incarnation, standing in for
        //    the reap-and-reallocate that pid_free + task_new perform, and
        //    that new incarnation dies. If the correlation were on the pid
        //    number alone, slot 21 would be torn down here — a LIVE
        //    process's authority destroyed on a number collision, which is
        //    worse than the leak this whole issue exists to close.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 15;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, 21;
        mov rsi, 1;                        // -> DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
        call driver_lifecycle_transition;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 21;
        mov rsi, r12;                      // pid 40 — live
        mov rdx, r13;                      // generation 1 — dead
        call driver_table_bind_pid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // The pid is recycled: a new occupant, a new incarnation.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call pid_gen_bump;
        cmp rax, r13;                      // must have MOVED
        je  pdw_fail;

        // The new occupant dies.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call driver_death_notify;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFC1;               // DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // The stale row is untouched: still bound, still Running, never
        // restarted.
        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
        cmp rax, 1;                        // DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_sup_incarnation;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // never re-armed
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // N. THE sys_exit ROUTE. No fault involved: sys_exit_body itself
        //    resolves the dying task to its row and runs the cascade.
        //
        //    Slot 22 is bound to pid 42, and pid 42 exits.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 16;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, 22;
        mov rsi, 42;
        lea rdx, [rip + pdw_name];
        xor rcx, rcx;
        call driver_table_register;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 22;
        mov rsi, 1;                        // -> DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
        call driver_lifecycle_transition;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 42;
        call pid_gen_bump;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 22;
        mov rsi, 42;
        call driver_death_bind;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 42;
        mov rdx, 1;                        // STATE_RUNNABLE
        call pdw_tcb_init;

        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        mov rsi, 7;                        // an ordinary exit status
        call sys_exit_body;

        // The row was torn down and re-armed by the exit alone.
        mov rdi, 22;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 22;
        call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // DRIVER_STATE_INIT
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 22;
        call driver_sup_incarnation;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // And the exit status is an ORDINARY one, not a fault-shaped one —
        // the two encodings must stay distinguishable to a reaping parent.
        lea rdi, [rip + _pdw_tcb];
        call pdw_exit_status;
        cmp rax, 7;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // P. THE UNBIND IS LOAD-BEARING ON ITS OWN.
        //
        //    Sub-tests G/K/L above are satisfied by EITHER of the two
        //    mechanisms that drop a binding: driver_death_notify's explicit
        //    unbind, or driver_table_rearm_incarnation dropping the key with
        //    the incarnation it named. Two independent mechanisms is the
        //    right design and the wrong test — an assertion both satisfy
        //    cannot tell you when one has been lost.
        //
        //    The ESCALATION path separates them. When the restart budget is
        //    exhausted, driver_restart_node latches perm_failed and returns
        //    WITHOUT re-arming — escalation is the absence of a re-arm, not
        //    a new state. So on that death, and only on that death, the
        //    re-arm cannot have dropped the key, and a binding that is gone
        //    afterwards is gone because the notifier consumed it.
        //
        //    Slot 20 already carries one restart from sub-test E. Four more
        //    deaths take the count to 5 (the intensity limit), and the sixth
        //    exceeds it.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 17;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov r14, 0;                        // deaths delivered in this loop

      pdw_burn_loop:
        cmp r14, 4;
        jae pdw_burn_done;

        mov rdi, 43;                       // a pid of its own
        call pid_gen_bump;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 43;
        call driver_death_bind;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 43;
        call driver_death_notify;
        cmp rax, 0;                        // DRV_RESTART_OK — still under
        jne pdw_fail;                      // budget

        add r14, 1;
        jmp pdw_burn_loop;

      pdw_burn_done:
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_restart_count;
        cmp rax, 5;                        // DRV_RESTART_MAX_INTENSITY
        jne pdw_fail;

        // The sixth death exceeds the budget.
        mov rdi, 43;
        call pid_gen_bump;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 43;
        call driver_death_bind;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, 43;
        call driver_death_notify;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFB3;               // DRV_RESTART_ESCALATED
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // Escalated: left in the terminal state, NOT re-armed. The
        // incarnation is unchanged from the fifth restart.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
        cmp rax, 5;                        // DRIVER_STATE_STOPPED
        jne pdw_fail;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_sup_perm_failed;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // AND YET THE BINDING IS GONE. No re-arm ran on this death, so
        // this is the notifier's own unbind and nothing else.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // Q. Idempotence at the escalated node. A second arrival must
        //    resolve NOTHING — not resolve the row and be turned away by
        //    the perm_failed gate. The difference matters: the second
        //    answer would then depend on a latch the supervisor can clear
        //    by unregistering and re-registering, and idempotence that
        //    evaporates when a latch is cleared is not idempotence.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 18;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        mov rdi, 43;
        call driver_death_notify;
        mov r15, 0xFFFFFFC1;               // DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT
        cmp rax, r15;
        jne pdw_fail;

        // ================================================================
        // O. Leave everything as we found it.
        // ================================================================
        mov rax, 19;
        lea rcx, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov [rcx], rax;

        call pdw_teardown;

        mov rdi, 3;                        // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
        lea rdx, [rip + death_witness_ok_msg];
        call klog_s1;

        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      pdw_fail:
        call pdw_teardown;

        lea rax, [rip + _pdw_stage];
        mov r8, [rax];
        mov rdi, 1;                        // LEVEL_ERROR
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
        lea rdx, [rip + death_witness_fail_msg];
        lea rcx, [rip + k_line];
        call klog_s1_d1;

        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdw_teardown() -> ()
  //
  // Restore _current_tcb and release every row and endpoint this fixture
  // touched. Reached from BOTH exits, so a failed assertion cannot leave the
  // scheduler pointed at fixture storage — which would turn one failed
  // sub-test into a boot that never finishes.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let pdw_teardown : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): restore _current_tcb from _pdw_saved_tcb and unregister slots 20/21/22 plus the endpoint this fixture allocated. Called from the success exit AND from pdw_fail, because the _current_tcb substitution is the one piece of fixture state that would harm the rest of boot if it outlived a failure: every subsequent syscall dispatch and every timer tick reads it, and leaving it pointed at _pdw_tcb would turn one failed assertion into a boot that never finishes. Three pushes (rbx/r12/r13) keep rsp %16 == 0 across the nested calls. Endpoint id is read back from _pdw_ep rather than a register so the teardown is correct from either exit regardless of what r14 held. driver_table_unregister zeroes the whole row including the pid binding, so no stale key survives into a later witness. Reserved labels: prefixed pdw_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;                          // 3 pushes -> rsp %16 = 0

        lea rax, [rip + _pdw_saved_tcb];
        mov rdi, [rax];
        call pdw_set_current;

        mov rdi, 20;
        call driver_table_unregister;
        mov rdi, 21;
        call driver_table_unregister;
        mov rdi, 22;
        call driver_table_unregister;

        lea rax, [rip + _pdw_ep];
        mov rdi, [rax];
        call endpoint_free;

        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
